     which means that if a regular expression matches another defined mount point,
     it will override the previously defined threshold.
 
+    A mount can also be given a ``min`` and/or ``max`` threshold instead of a
+    bare percentage. ``max`` behaves exactly like the plain percentage form
+    above, emitting a beacon when usage rises to or above it. ``min`` emits a
+    beacon when usage drops to or below it, which is useful for catching a
+    mount that silently failed and is actually reporting the usage of the
+    (typically much emptier) underlying root filesystem.
+
+    .. code-block:: yaml
+
+        beacons:
+          diskusage:
+            - /: 63%
+            - /mnt/nfs:
+                min: 15%
+                max: 80%
+            - /mnt/other:
+                min: 15%
+
